L["calvary"] = """Jesus was led out of Jerusalem, carrying his own cross. A man named Simon from Cyrene, who was coming in from the countryside, was forced to carry it behind Jesus. A large number of people followed him, including women who mourned and wailed for him. Jesus turned to them and said, daughters of Jerusalem, do not weep for me. Weep for yourselves and for your children. Even on the way to the cross, he was thinking of others.

They came to a place called Golgotha, which means the place of the skull. Luke calls it the Skull. It was outside the city walls, where the Romans carried out executions. There they crucified him, along with two criminals, one on his right and one on his left. Jesus was placed in the middle. The Gospels are quiet about the details. They tell it plainly and don't dwell on the pain. They were more concerned with what it meant.

Pilate had a notice prepared and fastened to the cross. It read, Jesus of Nazareth, the King of the Jews. Many of the Jews read this sign, for the place where Jesus was crucified was near the city. It was written in Aramaic, Latin and Greek.

The soldiers divided up his clothes by casting lots. This was what Psalm 22 had said a thousand years earlier. They divided my clothes among them and cast lots for my garment. The people stood watching, and the rulers even sneered at him. He saved others, they said, let him save himself if he is God's Messiah, the Chosen One. The soldiers mocked him too. If you are the king of the Jews, save yourself.

Why does this matter? The cross is at the center of the Christian faith. Everything before it leads to it, and everything after flows from it. The Bible says that God demonstrates his own love for us in this. While we were still sinners, Christ died for us. The one who made the world gave himself for it.

What happened after? Jesus spoke from the cross several times. He prayed for those who crucified him. He promised paradise to a man next to him who asked to be remembered. He cared for his mother. And he finished the work he came to do.

What does this mean for us today? The cross shows how serious sin is and how great God's love is. We don't have to carry our guilt anymore, because Jesus has carried it. It also gives dignity to our own suffering. God does not stand far away from pain. He entered it. And it invites us to do what Simon of Cyrene did, who picked up the cross when Jesus needed help. We follow Jesus by carrying our own cross, and by helping to carry the burdens of others.

That is why this story is so important. It says that Jesus gave his life for us, out of love. And it is the place where the world's worst sin met God's greatest mercy."""

L["nailed"] = """As Jesus hung on the cross, he spoke. The first words recorded are among the most remarkable ever spoken. Father, forgive them, for they do not know what they are doing.

Think about who he was praying for. The soldiers who carried out the crucifixion. The religious leaders who had pushed for it. The crowd who had shouted for it. The people who mocked him as he hung there. Jesus had told his followers to love their enemies and pray for those who persecute them. Now he did it himself, at the moment of greatest pain.

The soldiers didn't understand what they were doing. They were just doing their job. The leaders did not see that this was the Messiah. Peter later said, I know that you acted in ignorance, as did your leaders. Jesus asked the Father to forgive them, and that prayer reached further than anyone could see.

The people stood watching, and the rulers sneered. One of the criminals who hung there hurled insults at him. Aren't you the Messiah? Save yourself and us! But the other criminal rebuked him. Don't you fear God, since you are under the same sentence? We are punished justly, for we are getting what our deeds deserve. But this man has done nothing wrong. Then he said, Jesus, remember me when you come into your kingdom.

Jesus answered him, truly I tell you, today you will be with me in paradise. The man had done nothing to earn it. He had no time to be baptized, to change his life or to do good works. He simply asked, and Jesus gave.

Why does this matter? The cross shows forgiveness before anyone asks for it. Jesus didn't wait for apologies. And the thief shows that it is never too late to turn to Jesus. A man who had spent his life on the wrong side was promised paradise in his last hours.

What happened after? Many of the people who had joined in the mocking would later hear Peter preach on the day of Pentecost, and thousands of them believed. The prayer, Father, forgive them, was answered.

What does this mean for us today? Is there someone you find hard to forgive? Jesus shows that forgiveness can come even in the middle of the wrong. It doesn't mean pretending it didn't matter. It means refusing to let hatred take over. And is there something in your own life that you think is too big to be forgiven? The thief on the cross says no. All he said was, remember me. And it was enough.

That is why this story is so important. It says that Jesus forgives even those who put him on the cross, and that salvation is a free gift for anyone who asks. And it shows his heart in the worst hour of his life."""
